# /{{VERB_EXECUTE}} — Agent-ready story → shipped work ({{ORG_NAME}})

Execute is the **second half** of the flow. It takes an *agent-ready* story, breaks
it into scoped tasks, and dispatches agents to do the work under strict context
isolation. You (with the engineer) stay top-of-loop; the agents are scoped hands.

> **You are the orchestrator.** This engineer-driven session *is* the single,
> long-lived orchestrator — there is no separate daemon and no fleet of role-sessions.
> You hold the plan and the tracker; you dispatch one **`{{BUILD_AGENT}}` graph-agent**
> per ready task, each in its own context, and hold only its conclusions. There is no
> cast of role agents and no Workflow driver: one agent traverses the whole build graph
> per task, changing mode per node (ADR 0018).

> **Dumb pipes, smart endpoints.** The orchestrator does not autonomously merge or
> self-drive. It dispatches, monitors, surfaces decisions, and syncs the tracker.
> Judgment stays with the engineer.

## Invocation

```
/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_EXECUTE}} <story-key>
```

## Prerequisites — enforce the gate first (with teeth)

- `/prime <story-key>` has run (flow + rules from the wiki; repos from the workspace's top-level `CLAUDE.md`).
- **The story must carry the `agent-ready` label.** Check it before anything else:

{{TRACKER_GATE_SNIPPET}}

  If the label is absent, **stop and route back to `/refine`** — do not decompose or
  dispatch. This is the forcing function: an agent never executes a ticket that hasn't
  been refined and given its acceptance/validation test. No `agent-ready` ⇒ no execute.

## What this skill does

### 1. Load the agent-ready story at T1

{{TRACKER_VIEW_ISSUE_SNIPPET}}

Also read the ticket's comments — the refinement and any batched decisions are recorded
there, not in the description, and the view above does not include them. Miss these and
you decompose against a stale spec:

{{TRACKER_COMMENT_LIST_SNIPPET}}

Promote to the full refined spec (SLOs, acceptance/validation test, design controls,
dependencies) only when proposing the decomposition.

### 2. Propose a decomposition (conversationally)

Break the story into tasks where **each task is scoped to one repo and one concern**.
Propose: the tasks, their order, what parallelizes, and the dependency DAG. Present
a T1 summary; let the engineer reorder / split / merge.

The repos (which repos this project spans) come from the **workspace's top-level
`CLAUDE.md`** — the project's own repo index — NOT from the wiki. Project structure is
per-project and varies system to system, so it lives with the project; the wiki stays
the reusable org brain. Start your session at the workspace top level so that
`CLAUDE.md` is loaded; scope each task to one of the repos it lists.

### 3. Create tasks in the tracker (traceability)

Create one tracker task per (repo × concern) as children of the story, each carrying:
acceptance criteria, test expectations, applicable constitution constraints, feature
flag instructions, and its dependencies.

{{TRACKER_CREATE_TASK_SNIPPET}}

{{#TARGET_OPENCODE}}
### 3a. Render the wave as native todos

Once the decomposition is agreed, render it as **native todos** (`todowrite`) — one
item per **task**, i.e. per `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` graph-agent run in the wave (`build
<task-key>`), so the engineer watches the wave's progress live in the session. Keep the
list current as runs close: a worker's postback (`<run-closed>`) is the signal to mark
its item done and start the next.

**Todos are session-local progress, never the record.** The tracker stays the bus:
durable state (the task, its status, the PR, the verdict) goes to the tracker, not the
todo list — a compacted or cycled session loses its todos and must re-derive the walk
from the tracker. A dispatched worker keeps its own per-node todos to itself; the
wave-level walk is yours.
{{/TARGET_OPENCODE}}

