### 5. Dispatch one build graph-agent per ready task (single-locus, isolated context)

For each ready task, fire **one `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` graph-agent** via the native **Agent
tool** with `isolation: 'worktree'` (its contract is `agents/{{BUILD_AGENT}}.md`). It
traverses the whole graph — understand → build → validate → **review (self-check)** →
fix → clear — in **one persistent context**, and opens the PR. There is **no `Workflow`
and no `pipeline()`**: a coupled build → review → fix loop is one reasoning trajectory,
not a cast of stages handing off (ADR 0018).

Give it a context envelope containing **only**: the task spec, the target repo + branch,
test expectations, applicable constitution rules, and feature-flag instructions. Do
**not** pre-load sibling-task context — isolation keeps agents honest and cheap.

**Review is a self-check node of that one agent, not a second subagent.** The agent
re-reads its own diff adversarially against an explicit rubric before it opens the PR;
the relied-upon independent review is the **human merge gate + CI**. Do not dispatch a
separate reviewer into the loop.

**Fan out several build agents only for genuinely-independent tasks** — a valid DAG of
(repo × concern) is warranted decomposition; fire them in one turn and they run
concurrently. A single coupled task is never split across agents.

**Worktree isolation in a multi-repo workspace.** When a session runs from a *workspace
root* (whose subdirectories are the real git repos, the root itself not a repo),
`isolation: 'worktree'` can only create a worktree if it knows which repo to anchor in.
The harness's `WorktreeCreate` hook (`hooks/scripts/worktree-create.sh`) resolves the
target repo from the **agent label / worktree name**, so name each run `<repo>/<ticket>`
(or `<repo>--<ticket>`). Resolution order: cwd-is-a-repo → repo encoded in the name →
the `WORKTREE_REPO` env var → the sole git subdir if there's exactly one → else it
**fails closed** (it never guesses the wrong repo).

**Model floor for this org:** default **{{MODEL_POLICY_DEFAULT}}**; banned:
**{{MODEL_POLICY_BANNED}}**. {{MODEL_POLICY_RULE}} The `{{BUILD_AGENT}}` agent pins its
model + effort in its own frontmatter, so every run lands at the right depth; you never
set effort for it by hand. We optimize for value-per-token, not cheapest token.
{{#SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}

**Optional supplementary reviewer (high-risk diffs only).** For a high-risk diff you may
dispatch a **fresh-context reviewer** on the **completed** PR — a second Agent that reads
the finished artifact and returns a verdict. It never interleaves inside the build →
review → fix loop (that loop is never split). This is the exception, not the standard
graph.
{{/SUPP_REVIEWER_ENABLED}}
{{/TARGET_CC}}
